{
  "summary": "Adds a scheduled GitHub Actions workflow that runs weekly to inspect the Windows code signing certificate. If the certificate is within 90 days of expiry or already expired, the workflow automatically files a GitHub issue with the certificate details and relevant commit. It suppresses duplicate issues by checking for existing closed or open reports matching the target expiration date.",
  "problem": "There is no automated check for code signing certificate expiration. Maintainers currently notice expired certificates during release candidate builds, which risks delaying urgent or short-notice bugfix releases while waiting for renewals.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author notes: \"Currently we have no automation in place to check that our code signing certificates are not expired or close to expiry. We have previously 'relied on Release Candidates' as a dry-run for codesigners to realise that a certificate has expired and have time to fix it. 'Noticing during RC cycle' also might not be appropriate in the future if we release more bugfix minor/patch releases on short notice.\""
  ],
